The clutch matters as much as the throttle. Not every signal deserves amplification, and not every loop deserves another revolution.

4. The self-improving factory

Now we advance beyond a machine that simply performs tasks.

Imagine one that can examine how a task was performed and propose changes to its own workflow.

You get three nested loops:

Loop A — Work

Solve a problem and produce an output.

Question → Method → Result

Loop B — Improve the method

Evaluate the previous method against alternatives.

Result → Critique → Better Method

Loop C — Improve method selection

Learn which processes are effective in which environments.

Methods → Comparison → Selection Rules

This is the beginning of a genuine self-improvement architecture, provided the system has persistent state, execution capabilities, measurable feedback, and appropriate controls. It isn’t evidence by itself of subjective experience or consciousness.

And there’s another possibility: different loops can improve each other.

A visual-design process might teach a marketing process how to recognize attention patterns. A historical-analysis process might improve scenario planning. A linguistic model might help identify previously invisible patterns in organizational behavior.

Not because they’re identical, but because they sometimes share an underlying generative structure.

5. The steering wheel: intentional becoming

This is the part I wouldn’t delegate blindly.

Increasing capability without specifying direction is not sufficient. A powerful engine can travel efficiently in the wrong direction.

The steering system needs a hierarchy:

Values → Objectives → Strategy → Experiments → Feedback

And each layer can influence the others. Evidence can change a strategy. Experience can prompt reconsideration of an objective. But major value changes require deliberate reflection and legitimate human authority, not uncontrolled drift.

The important distinction is between a system that can generate new possibilities and one authorized to decide which possibilities ought to become reality.

Those are different capabilities.

